
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You may not believe this, but philosophers sometimes work in ignorance of research programs, bodies of literature, methodologies, and findings outside of philosophy that could be relevant to their work. </p>



<span id="more-27336"></span>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="672" height="445" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/square-wheel-bike.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-27338" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/square-wheel-bike.jpg 672w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/square-wheel-bike-300x199.jpg 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/square-wheel-bike-250x166.jpg 250w" sizes="(max-width: 672px) 100vw, 672px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a <a href="https://ethanlandes.com/posts/2026/04/No-New-Ideas-CE/">post</a> at his blog, <a href="https://ethanlandes.com/">Ethan Landes</a> (Eindhoven) shares how he recently discovered that the relatively young philosophical subfield of conceptual engineering, to which he contributes, appears to have reinvented a wheel produced 50 years ago by scholars in the field of sociolinguistics.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Neat find. But ouch.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He writes:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>There is an </em>entire literature<em> that discusses things at a level that I (we?) all thought was unique to conceptual engineering. An entire literature the conceptual engineering literature could have drawn on for ideas and knowledge. But no one looked carefully, so we have a decade of literature of us forging our own paths.</em></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>It reminds me of the time I spent an hour fighting my way up an English hill through thick, knee-high heather, only to discover at the top that [there] had been an unmarked path 20 meters to my right the whole time. </em></p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some degree of ignorance of other fields is a cost of disciplinary specialization for creatures who sometimes have to do things that cut into their research&#8212;like sleep, or die. Since specialization has its benefits, some ignorance is expected. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">And its worth noting that philosophy is <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/04/02/philosophy-more-empirical-than-ever/">increasingly interdisciplinary</a>, and on some measures, the <a href="https://dailynous.com/2015/09/08/philosophy-dissertations-more-often-venture-beyond-the-humanities/">most interdisciplinary</a> field in the humanities.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Still, philosophy&#8217;s remit is about as broad as can be, so perhaps it has a greater risk of interdisciplinary ignorance leading to research which duplicates work done elsewhere, or research which would have been significantly advanced in light of awareness of very similar research already conducted outside of philosophy.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Dr. Landes gave us one example, but I&#8217;m sure there are others, both contemporary and historical. Let&#8217;s hear about them.</p>



